<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Unsandboxed, deliberately — but not for the reason this comment used to
	  give. It claimed the Rust core needs the database path without "a
	  container dance". It does not: every path is derived from
	  `.applicationSupportDirectory` and `.temporaryDirectory` and handed to
	  the engine as an absolute path, and both of those resolve inside the
	  container for a sandboxed app. A container is an ordinary writable
	  directory, so the engine would not change at all.

	  The sandbox is required only for the Mac App Store. Klinote is
	  distributed directly with a Developer ID, where it is optional and
	  shipping without it is unremarkable.

	  What enabling it would actually take, so the next person does not have to
	  work it out again:
	    · the database, the models and the learned terms move to
	      ~/Library/Containers/one.klinote.mac/Data, so existing installs need
	      a migration — get it wrong and the notes are unreadable;
	    · the Keychain item may need re-authorising, because the sandbox scopes
	      keychain access to the app;
	    · the model downloader needs `com.apple.security.network.client`;
	    · the `scribe-llm` helper has to be re-verified under the sandbox.

	  It is the cheapest it will ever be while there are no installs to
	  migrate. Tracked in docs/compliance/PRIVACY.md.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<false/>
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
